                <pb facs="0779" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg683"/>

                <head>No. 433.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bingham to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation, Japan</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tokei</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-06-03"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 3, 1874</date>.
                        (Received July 6.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 88.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As will appear by the inclosure, herewith,
                    addressed to me by Mr. Dickins, as counsel, A. C. Dunn, a citizen of the United
                    States, has instituted suit for breach of contract against certain Japanese
                    subjects in a Japanese tribunal, which suit is still pending. It further appears
                    from this letter that the Japanese court having jurisdiction of the case refuses
                    to Mr. Dunn, the plaintiff, process to compel the attendance of his witnesses.
                    It would seem that this refusal is in flagrant disregard of article 6 of the
                    treaty of 1858, which provides that the Japanese courts shall be open to
                    American citizens for the recovery of their just claims against the Japanese.
                    When it is considered that the limits to travel to foreigners are denned and
                    restricted under the treaty, this refusal of the court to issue and execute
                    process, especially outside of the concession to foreigners, might be called a
                    denial of justice.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as this is a private contest, over which I have no official
                    jurisdiction, I have thought it best, before taking any action with the
                    government, to ask your instructions for the government of my course, not only
                    in this, but also in all other like cases that may arise.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JNO. A. BINGHAM.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d435-a1.xml" xpointer="d435-a1"/>
            </div>
